## Evaluate every important workflow

| Question | Must be yes |
| --- | --- |
| What does the user understand immediately? | Purpose and context clear |
| What do they believe happens next? | Primary action obvious |
| Is every primary action obvious? | One dominant action per screen |
| Does every action produce immediate feedback? | Acknowledgment within 100ms feel |
| Does navigation preserve context? | User not lost after transitions |
| Is progress visible? | Long tasks show state |
| Is system status understandable? | Loading, saving, errors legible |
| Does the interface prevent mistakes? | Confirm destructive; disable with reason |
| Can mistakes be recovered? | Undo, retry, preserved input |
| Is advanced capability progressively disclosed? | Power features not dumped on day one |
| Are new users guided without patronizing experts? | Skippable help; shortcuts for power users |
| Does the product feel responsive at all times? | See `perceived-performance.mdc` |
| Does value communicate during critical journeys? | Not only on marketing |
| Is the interface coherent when data is sparse, dense, delayed, missing, or wrong? | All states designed |

## Required workflow anatomy

Every important workflow must have:

1. Clear entry point
2. Visible current state
3. Clear primary action
4. Immediate interaction feedback
5. Progress communication (when work takes time)
6. Meaningful completion state
7. Recovery path
8. Logical next step

Reject interfaces that only look polished in the ideal happy-path screenshot.

